  <section class="wl-choose">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="wl-choose-header" data-animate>
        <span class="section-label">Finding the Right Fit</span>
        <h2 class="section-heading">
          How to Choose a White<br />
          Label SEO Provider
        </h2>
        <p class="section-subtext" style="margin:0 auto;">
          The right partner is the difference between a service you can sell
          confidently and one you regret quoting. Some questions you'll only
          get good answers to by asking directly. Here's what we recommend
          looking at.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="wl-choose-grid" data-stagger>
        <div class="wl-choose-card" data-animate>
          <div class="wl-choose-number">01</div>
          <h3 class="wl-choose-title">Choose a specialist, not a generalist</h3>
          <p class="wl-choose-desc">
            The shops that only do SEO build better processes, set sharper
            pricing, and communicate more clearly than full-service agencies
            that bolt SEO on as one of ten services. Specialization compounds.
            We only do SEO, which is part of why our process is built around
            running dozens of campaigns concurrently without quality drift.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="wl-choose-card" data-animate>
          <div class="wl-choose-number">02</div>
          <h3 class="wl-choose-title">Walk both onboarding processes</h3>
          <p class="wl-choose-desc">
            Two onboardings matter. Yours as a partner (how do you get up and
            running with them?) and your client's (what happens in the first
            30 days of a campaign?). A good provider can walk you through
            both in detail without hesitating. We love sharing ours because
            we've sweated the details.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="wl-choose-card" data-animate>
          <div class="wl-choose-number">03</div>
          <h3 class="wl-choose-title">Meet the actual delivery team</h3>
          <p class="wl-choose-desc">
            Sales and delivery are not the same people. Before you commit,
            get face time with the account managers and strategists who will
            actually run your campaigns. At SEO Brothers, "meet the team"
            calls are open to any prospective partner at any time.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="wl-choose-card" data-animate>
          <div class="wl-choose-number">04</div>
          <h3 class="wl-choose-title">Ask for sample deliverables</h3>
          <p class="wl-choose-desc">
            What do reports look like? What do dashboards look like? What's
            actually being worked on inside their SEO initiatives each
            month? Examples are easier to evaluate than promises. Providers
            that won't share sample work usually have a reason.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="wl-choose-card" data-animate>
          <div class="wl-choose-number">05</div>
          <h3 class="wl-choose-title">Understand the link methodology</h3>
          <p class="wl-choose-desc">
            Link quality varies wildly. Ask about the types of placements,
            how outreach happens, and what the editorial standards are.
            Then ask what they refuse to do. A provider with no "won't do"
            list is one to be cautious of.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="wl-choose-card" data-animate>
          <div class="wl-choose-number">06</div>
          <h3 class="wl-choose-title">Brand protection and contract flexibility</h3>
          <p class="wl-choose-desc">
            Your clients are your clients. The contract should say so
            explicitly: no poaching, no direct contact, work and assets
            belong to your agency. And terms should let you scale month to
            month. Anyone asking for a year-long lock-in is hedging their
            own quality concerns.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== COMMON PITFALLS ===== -->
  <section class="wl-pitfalls">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="wl-pitfalls-header" data-animate>
        <span class="section-label">What to Watch For</span>
        <h2 class="section-heading">
          Common White Label SEO<br />
          Pitfalls to Avoid
        </h2>
        <p class="section-subtext" style="margin:0 auto;">
          Most white label partnerships go wrong for the same handful of
          reasons. None of them are subtle. All of them are visible in the
          discovery call if you know what to ask.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="wl-pitfalls-grid" data-stagger>
        <div class="wl-pitfall-card" data-animate>
          <div class="wl-pitfall-marker">01</div>
          <h3 class="wl-pitfall-title">Opaque delivery timelines</h3>
          <p class="wl-pitfall-desc">
            "We'll get to it" is not a delivery timeline. A real provider
            can tell you what gets done in week one, week four, and month
            three. If the timeline is fuzzy at sign-up, it stays fuzzy.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="wl-pitfall-card" data-animate>
          <div class="wl-pitfall-marker">02</div>
          <h3 class="wl-pitfall-title">Reporting that doesn't reach your brand</h3>
          <p class="wl-pitfall-desc">
            Some "white label" providers will brand the cover page and call
            it done while their logo lives in the footer of every chart.
            Look at a real report end to end before signing. Your brand
            belongs everywhere or it's not white label.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="wl-pitfall-card" data-animate>
          <div class="wl-pitfall-marker">03</div>
          <h3 class="wl-pitfall-title">Vague link methodology</h3>
          <p class="wl-pitfall-desc">
            Specific source domains are proprietary at most reputable
            providers (publishing the full list publicly tanks the value).
            But the methodology shouldn't be a mystery. A good provider
            explains placement types, editorial standards, what they refuse
            to do, and can show sample placements. If all you get is
            "trust us, the links are great," walk.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="wl-pitfall-card" data-animate>
          <div class="wl-pitfall-marker">04</div>
          <h3 class="wl-pitfall-title">Contracts that lock you in</h3>
          <p class="wl-pitfall-desc">
            A long lock-in is a vote of low confidence from the provider.
            If they're not earning the relationship month by month, they
            shouldn't be paid for months they didn't earn. Look for
            month-to-month terms, or at minimum clear exit provisions.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="wl-pitfall-card" data-animate>
          <div class="wl-pitfall-marker">05</div>
          <h3 class="wl-pitfall-title">No defined client ownership</h3>
          <p class="wl-pitfall-desc">
            The work, content, and links your provider creates should
            belong to your agency without question. Get that in writing
            before the campaign starts, not after a client tries to leave.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

